WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.160
How do you describe a Magic: the Gathering deck?&nbsp;
Experienced players have a rich lexicon to draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.040
upon while talking to each other about decks—this&nbsp;
deck I’m playing is a red-white aggro deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.720
it’s a burn deck in boros colors, a sligh&nbsp;
variant existing within the Lorehold color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.600
identity if you wanna get real creative with your&nbsp;
terminology. Certainly, players can have different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.240
interpretations of these terms, and arguments&nbsp;
about semantics do pop up from time to time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.760
but ultimately as players we’re more on the&nbsp;
same page than we think. Where things get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.360
tricky is when it comes time to talk to newer&nbsp;
players, or even people with little to no MTG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.880
experience to speak of. How do you describe&nbsp;
midrange to somebody who is unfamiliar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:45.320
the concept? It’s doable but it can be a little&nbsp;
tricky, and overloading somebody with too many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.720
abstract pieces of terminology isn’t the best&nbsp;
way to build up a practical understanding of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.760
the game. This is where simple archetypes end&nbsp;
up being very useful. A lot of players start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:00.560
out by building around mechanics or keywords—my&nbsp;
first deck was a mono-black bloodthirst deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.440
which I played against my brother’s mana ramp&nbsp;
deck and my cousin’s spellslinger deck. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.200
by far the most approachable of&nbsp;
these types of decks is tribal decks.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.760
When I tell somebody I’m playing a zombies deck&nbsp;
or a dragons deck, most novice magic players will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.840
understand roughly what sort of deck I’m talking&nbsp;
about, and there will even be plenty of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.960
who don’t play the game who can follow along.&nbsp;
It helps that tribes usually match mechanically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.800
with broad cultural understandings—zombies are&nbsp;
a horde focused around death, dragons are big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.400
flying beasts that shoot fire, knights are&nbsp;
warriors who prefer fair and direct combat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:42.440
ninjas are skilled at getting in and out of places&nbsp;
sneakily, slivers are—okay it’s not perfect but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:48.240
it’s pretty good. I’d even say that tribal decks&nbsp;
perform an especially important function in EDH.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.360
Limited events and 60 card kitchen table magic&nbsp;
are probably better ways to learn the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.720
but for better or worse EDH has become a big&nbsp;
place of onboarding for newer players within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:02.240
the landscape of paper magic, and tribal decks&nbsp;
are one of the best options for learning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.680
game within that context. While lots of&nbsp;
EDH decks have high levels of complexity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:11.160
with the structural elements that hold a deck&nbsp;
together somewhat obscured to an untrained eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.560
tribal decks leave large portions of their&nbsp;
synergies bare and on the table. To check if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.840
a card synergizes with Hakbal of the Surging&nbsp;
Soul, I don’t need to look at anything but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:26.640
the type box on a card—if it’s a Merfolk, it’s&nbsp;
worth consideration. These decks are also usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.640
straightforward to play compared to non-tribal&nbsp;
decks—simply play cards of that tribal type,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:36.360
interspersed with lords to maximize the value from&nbsp;
those cards, a category which usually includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.440
the commander. A simpler gameplan means less to&nbsp;
think about on a player’s own side of the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.000
and offers more time and mental space for a&nbsp;
newer player to strategize, pay attention to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.840
cards their opponents are playing, and get&nbsp;
a better feeling for the format as a whole.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.320
So yeah, I’m a fan of tribal decks on the whole,&nbsp;
particularly in the way they offer a smooth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.120
satisfying experience for newer players. I&nbsp;
also don’t say any of this to insult more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.280
experienced players who like tribal decks—I’ve&nbsp;
built a number of tribal decks over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:08.720
and my current strongest deck is a tribal&nbsp;
deck. However, I think a lot of tribal decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.840
end up falling into some lazy deckbuilding&nbsp;
patterns, even when they’re constructed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:17.160
players with a decent amount of experience&nbsp;
under their belt. Just as having a core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.360
creature type can be a helpful hand to hold&nbsp;
for newer players, it can be a real limiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.600
factor if a player is unable to break out of&nbsp;
that mold. On the surface Zombies, Goblins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:33.160
and Dragons all sound like valid gameplans,&nbsp;
but are they actual plans for playing the game?

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.440
Let’s take a step back for a moment. I’m going to&nbsp;
make two statements, the first of which I expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.760
to be fairly uncontroversial, and the second&nbsp;
of which might be a bit more challenging to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:48.240
people. Statement one: not all tribal cards are&nbsp;
good in every tribal deck. Kargan Dragonrider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.400
though it is more powerful in a dragon deck than a&nbsp;
non-dragon deck, still probably isn’t a card that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:59.200
most dragon players want. Statement two: not all&nbsp;
tribal cards that are good on paper are good in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:04.760
every tribal deck. For a couple examples of this,&nbsp;
I’m going to reference the EDHRec page of Miirym,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:09.880
one of the most popular tribal commanders.&nbsp;
One card that jumps out is Crucible of Fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.960
played in 22% of decks. On paper,&nbsp;
this card is amazing. The going rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.960
for a basic anthem is 3 mana, maybe 2 mana if&nbsp;
you’re restricting the creatures it affects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:25.720
and by that metric this card is giving 6 mana&nbsp;
worth of anthem for 4 mana—that’s great! Lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.520
of tribal decks would happily accept that kind of&nbsp;
efficiency, and a good number of green black elf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:37.280
decks are happy to run a card that is far worse on&nbsp;
an objective level. So what’s the problem? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:43.960
anthems are an awkward choice in a dragon deck&nbsp;
because it’s not a go-wide deck. Realistically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:48.840
you’re going to have a low single digits number of&nbsp;
dragons a large portion of the time. There aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.320
that many low cost dragons nor many cards that&nbsp;
produce dragon tokens outside of Miirym, so if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.160
you have a big enough board to make an anthem&nbsp;
good you were probably already headed either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:05.320
toward victory or toward a board wipe. An even&nbsp;
more questionable choice is Acolyte of Bahamut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:11.320
played in 34% of Miirym decks. This enchantment&nbsp;
provides ramp at a decent rate, and it’s a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:15.520
fit in a Korlessa deck, where the commander&nbsp;
is two mana and has a somewhat modest effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.760
that won’t attract too much removal. However, if&nbsp;
you’ve ever played with Miirym you know that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:26.080
the Miirym player is allowed to untap with her in&nbsp;
play, an avalanche of bad things will frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.240
follow for the other players at the table. And&nbsp;
your opponents need to allow you to untap twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:38.680
with your terrifying 6 mana commander before&nbsp;
Acolyte, a ramp card, has become mana-positive.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.800
To me, this card is a clear instance of one&nbsp;
of the more pernicious side effects of tribal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.480
deckbuilding. Just as it’s helpful for newer&nbsp;
players to be able to rapidly analyze cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:52.760
based on having and mentioning a card type, as&nbsp;
players gain experience this sort of instinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:58.080
often gets encoded as a heuristic to follow if&nbsp;
they aren’t actively pushing back against it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.880
If a card does theoretically helpful things&nbsp;
on a theoretically high enough power level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:08.960
players’ brains are often tuned to shortcut a lot&nbsp;
of the details, details that really matter. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:14.960
surface, if I’m a Miirym player looking at Acolyte&nbsp;
of Bahamut, it’s doing all the things I want—it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:19.480
nice for my expensive dragons to be cheaper,&nbsp;
and I really like my commander so most games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:24.360
I’ll have it in play anyway. It’s only when we&nbsp;
deconstruct what Miirym does as a commander, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:30.040
a dragon deck is actually trying to do, and what a&nbsp;
Miirym dragon deck’s strengths and weaknesses are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:36.000
that the card’s issues start to become obvious.&nbsp;
And when that sort of analysis doesn’t happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.120
thousands of players end up keeping a&nbsp;
really mediocre win-more card in their deck.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:45.280
Okay, so I’ve laid out some tribal deckbuilding&nbsp;
habits that I find to be problematic and which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.440
lead to decks that function worse. With that in&nbsp;
mind, how does one make a tribal deck that feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:55.160
cohesive and effective? Fundamentally,&nbsp;
it comes down to developing an identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:59.720
and a gameplan for your deck that’s more&nbsp;
complex than “play insert creature type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.800
here”. That can be a difficult process,&nbsp;
but a good way to start tackling that task&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.400
is looking at your core tribal cards and asking&nbsp;
yourself “what is this offering me? What sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:15.120
gameplan might play well with these cards?” I’ve&nbsp;
built a number of tribal decks over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.800
and I’m going to reference some of the more&nbsp;
successful ones as examples for this. One such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:25.040
example is my high power pirates deck—I picked&nbsp;
out a command zone with a couple pseudo-lords,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.600
started with a calculated number of low-cost&nbsp;
pirates to utilize their effect, and then added&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:34.160
additional pirates and pirate-tribal elements&nbsp;
on an individual basis where I thought it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:39.080
worthwhile to my general gameplan to do so. A&nbsp;
large part of the deck’s gameplan features the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:44.840
tribe and tribal creature beatdown is a genuine&nbsp;
wincon, but there are also a number of layers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:49.840
of the deck that diverge from the nature of a&nbsp;
pure tribal deck, mainly cards centered around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:55.280
tribal payoffs such as the treasures that Malcolm&nbsp;
generates. To me, this deck checks all the boxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:59.760
of what good tribal deckbuilding should look&nbsp;
like, but this deck is decidedly on a higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.840
power level than most tribal decks, so I’ll&nbsp;
also mention a couple lower-power examples.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:10.480
One of the decks in the $25 deck pool I’ve talked&nbsp;
about was an Arahbo cats deck—don’t talk to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.880
the card was like a dollar when I built the&nbsp;
deck in 2020. The deck isn’t that weird from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.400
a composition standpoint—it’s running a few&nbsp;
of the affordable cat lords and a moderate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:25.400
overall creature count of 27—but it nonetheless&nbsp;
is built with a more specific mindset than just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:31.120
“play cats”. The commander’s eminence gives&nbsp;
a single cat +3/+3 until end of turn on each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:37.160
of your combat phases, and already this diverges&nbsp;
from the conventional tribal deck mold—rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:43.120
rewarding a critical mass of cats, this commander&nbsp;
rewards having a single cat early in the game to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:48.880
get maximum use out of the eminence ability. The&nbsp;
commander’s on board effect is an amplification of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:55.120
this—it rewards having a small quantity of large&nbsp;
cats, since each cat buffed requires spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.120 --> 00:09:00.840
3 mana. For this reason, the deck’s creatures&nbsp;
are lower-cost than ones in a lot of cat decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:05.680
and it runs a lower creature count than average&nbsp;
in order to have a healthy suite of equipments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:10.520
and a number of draw spells which check the&nbsp;
power of a creature on board. In this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:15.720
the deck dips into aggro and voltron themes in&nbsp;
addition to being a midrange-y tribal deck, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:20.520
this extra attention paid to being cohesive with&nbsp;
the tribal core allowed the deck to end up feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:26.480
a lot more focused and threatening than most&nbsp;
budget tribal decks. As a final example, sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:32.280
I’ll stumble accidentally into a tribe. When I&nbsp;
built a rogue tribal deck for the $25 deck pool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:37.440
I didn’t actually start with the idea of it being&nbsp;
a tribal deck. Rather, I built out the core of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.720
deck with a focus on evasive creatures, curiosity&nbsp;
effects, and a high density of interaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.795
and then went “wow I have a lot of rogues in this&nbsp;
deck” when I stopped to look at what I’d built so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.795 --> 00:09:51.200
far. From there, I added the rogue-tribal cards&nbsp;
which made sense with the original gameplan, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.360
was actually a fair number since the tribe as a&nbsp;
whole is heavily based around evasive creatures.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:00.520
Basically, what I’m trying to convey here is just&nbsp;
that a key step in developing a tribal deck is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:05.720
building an identity for it outside of the tribe&nbsp;
alone, and finding that identity will work best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:11.040
when it focuses around the core pillars of the&nbsp;
deck’s gameplan. Now, I do get the sense there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:15.560
may be a lot of you that are feeling pretty safe&nbsp;
right now, shielded from what I’m saying in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:19.480
video by the fact that you don’t play tribal&nbsp;
decks. It’s at this point that I feel this need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:25.960
to tell you that most of these points also apply&nbsp;
to any deck based around a specific mechanic or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:31.720
a set of words such as “enter the battlefield” or&nbsp;
“cast from exile”. If you make a deck by throwing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:36.720
together a big pile of stuff focused around that&nbsp;
mechanic without considering a broader gameplan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.920
your deck is probably going to feel like an&nbsp;
unfocused mess for purposes of closing out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:46.280
game. I talked about this more in my wincons&nbsp;
video, but basically when a player chases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:51.160
a particular mechanic or tribe rather than&nbsp;
chasing a gameplan for their deck to follow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.440
they often end up with a pile of different payoff&nbsp;
effects that all contribute toward different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.280
goals. In addition, lots of potentially&nbsp;
helpful cards may be left by the wayside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:04.200
with that deckspace instead occupied by filler&nbsp;
that doesn’t really contribute meaningfully to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:09.280
what the deck’s synergistic elements are actually&nbsp;
accomplishing. This kinda sucks on some level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:14.040
because this game has something like 30,000&nbsp;
cards and finding the right cards for a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:18.800
out of all of those would be a much easier process&nbsp;
if it could start and end with a single gatherer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:24.640
search. And honestly, I think that vastness of&nbsp;
cards alone is a valid reason to use a tribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:29.560
a mechanic, or a few words as a starting&nbsp;
point to build upon. Just keep in mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:33.840
that it is a starting point at the end of the&nbsp;
day, and if that’s the point you’re at you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:39.480
be well-served to focus on what’s at the heart of&nbsp;
your deck and figure out how to better serve that.

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:43.360
Thanks for watching! I’m currently taking&nbsp;
submissions for my Patron deck review this month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:46.360
so if you have an extra $3 a month&nbsp;
to throw at me and want a chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:50.480
to have me review your deck, a link&nbsp;
to my Patreon is in the description.

